WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:19.020
MALLABY: OK, I think we can get started.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.560
For those of you who don't know me, I'm Sebastian
Mallaby.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.010
And I work here at the Council.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.740
Welcome to today's meeting on the World Economic
Update.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.220
This will be on the record.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:37.350
And it's presented by the Maurice Greenberg
Center for Geoeconomic Studies.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.410
We're going to get going with a panel of three
speakers.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:46.550
On the far-my far left, your far right, as
he ruefully noted earlier, is James Grant,

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.220
founder and editor of Grant's Interest Rate
Observer.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:56.839
Emma Dinsmore in the middle, cofounder and
chief executive of R-Squared Macro, which

00:00:56.839 --> 00:00:59.339
is the hedge fund in Alabama, or a hedge fund?

00:00:59.339 --> 00:01:00.339
DINSMORE: The?

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:01.339
MALLABY: It could be the.

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:02.339
DINSMORE: The.

00:01:02.339 --> 00:01:04.360
MALLABY: The hedge fund in Alabama, OK.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.660
(Laughter.)

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:11.440
And next to me, Lewis Alexander, managing
director and chief-sorry-a managing director

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.570
and chief U.S. economist for Nomura Securities
International.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:25.780
So I think it's hard to not start with Brexit,
which was roiling the markets, especially

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.030
yesterday.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.920
And maybe, Jim, I'll start with you.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:36.850
There have been a whole queue of sort of blue
chip economists forecasting how much growth

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:43.950
Britain would forego if indeed the country
voted to leave the European Union.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:49.640
The latest person to intervene is George Soros,
who predicts that you'd have a currency fall

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:55.360
bigger than in 1992 when he made all that
money by shorting sterling.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.920
How do you see the-I mean, clearly there's
a negative effect from leaving Europe.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.140
How big do you think it would be?

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.740
Would it be in the currency?

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.979
What do you think?

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:07.979
GRANT: Good morning, Sebastian.

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:09.560
Good morning ladies and gentlemen.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:15.510
I would say, in the first place, that of all
the prognosticators on the future of this

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:20.959
British referendum, I dare say that George
Soros speaks with unique authority, certainly

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:27.620
with respect to the pound, and even more with
respect to the downside of the pound sterling.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:34.200
But, Sebastian, with respect to the myriad
forecasts having to do with the imminent loss

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:41.159
of this and that in Britain, in the event
that leave should win, I would say that-I

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:45.120
would ask of those people who have made these
predictions, including the British Treasury,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.069
the governor of the Bank of England, and on
and on, how many of them were on the right

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:55.220
side of the events of 2007 and '(0)8?

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.220
(Laughter.)

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.560
I would say the future is a closed book.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:06.209
And if all the economists think one thing,
one is at least advised to imagine the alternative

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:08.280
outcome.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.239
It seems to me that fundamentally the question
is not one of finance or of the future of

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:20.370
growth in Britain, but rather of a philosophical
nature.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:26.391
When Margaret Thatcher was dealing with the
events of the late '70s and early '80s, she

00:03:26.391 --> 00:03:33.170
was accosted by the members of her party about
the incomes policies that she had reviled.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.370
And they said: Margaret, you cannot do without
them.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:43.371
If the government does not lead industrialists
and unions in the direction-and you indicate

00:03:43.371 --> 00:03:48.269
a proper direction for growth, we will be
in a very bad way.

00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:53.059
And she said, in effect, my platform is freedom.

00:03:53.059 --> 00:04:00.739
My platform is supply and demand and the individuals
making choices in markets.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:05.689
So between the economists predicting disaster
on the occasion of a leave vote on the one

00:04:05.689 --> 00:04:13.019
hand, and as the alternative of perhaps a
greater increment of freedom, a greater increment

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:19.780
of opportunity-entrepreneurial opportunity,
I would say, it's at least appealing to give

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.150
some thought to that latter.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:27.250
MALLABY: So Margaret Thatcher was also, of
course, a supporter of EU membership, although

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.730
under terms which were tough that she negotiated.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:36.850
Maybe, Emma, so, you know, I guess Soros makes
three comparisons with 1992.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:46.090
He says that when sterling came out of the
exchange rate system then, at least when it

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.130
fell it boosted exports.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:53.490
In this case, if it falls the export boost
will be muted, to say the least, by the fact

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:57.510
that you're leaving a trade agreement, or
you may be leaving that trade agreement at

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.510
the same time.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.000
There's a bigger current account deficit now
in Britain so more of a reliance on foreign

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.130
capital.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:10.760
And monetary policy is more constrained because
interest rates are already so low.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:15.400
As somebody who trades macro how do you-do
you think that's right?

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.960
Do you think that the risks of a downside
shoot in sterling that could really be a Lehman

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.760
moment for Europe are there?

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.130
DINSMORE: I think it's really important to
put it in context of what's going on.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:33.170
In terms of the depreciation of the pound,
I think certainly a Brexit is a huge headwind

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.690
to the pound.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:38.230
Soros is estimating maybe 15 to 20 percent
decline in the pound.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:41.180
I think maybe 10 to 15 seems reasonable.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.400
You've seen that across the board with economists.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:49.430
Mark Carney at the BOE has said that the pound
would be very vulnerable in that sort of scenario.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.320
In terms of the trade implications, I think
they're somewhat muted by the fact that the

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:57.380
euro is likely to get hit as well, and any
kind of cross border trade there, there might

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:00.680
be a little bit of a wash, although I assume
that the pound will get hit greater than the

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.430
euro.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.740
And that creates tremendous uncertainty.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:11.370
I think beyond that, if Brexit passes, we're
looking at three years of negotiation for

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:17.220
the U.K. to come up with its own trade agreements,
which the U.K. has not unilaterally negotiated

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.790
a trade agreement since the 1970s when it
joined the EU.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:26.020
It doesn't even have those infrastructures
in place to do that.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:31.790
So I think that that-the uncertainty on that
outlook alone, what that means truly for the

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:39.370
U.K. economy, what the dynamics of the new
trade agreements will be, how it will affect

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:46.090
exports in an environment where global trade
is already dampened, really certainly present

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.390
headwinds for the pound.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.970
I expect credit spreads would likely blow
out.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.050
But I don't know if you would see a Lehman
size event.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:57.030
I think more likely you'd see something more
akin to what we saw with the European financial

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.090
crisis.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:04.250
And really, the response is going to depend
on how credible central banks handle it.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:05.480
I think that they're well-prepared.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.920
I think that there's been a lot of cooperation
among central banks.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:16.800
I believe that the Fed's decision to delay
rate normalization clearly influenced by Brexit.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.280
There's clearly a lot of preparation on that
front.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.640
So the extent there's some credibility in
there and a swift policy response, you might

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:23.730
need some of that.

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.140
But definitely short term volatility.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:30.660
MALLABY: And, Lewis, the idea of a central
bank policy response does run up against the

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.300
issue of two things.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:37.470
First of all, the zero lower bound is not
that far off, so how much can you really cut?

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.690
And particularly how much can you cut without
hurting U.K. banks?

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:47.100
And secondly, you know, in an environment
where you have an enormous current account

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:53.570
deficit, you need to attract capital, cutting
rates is obviously a problematic strategy.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:59.010
So do you-I mean, the Lehman idea, I guess,
is that you have this shock.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.260
People are not expecting it.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:01.260
And that's true.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:06.160
After yesterday, they're not expecting it
because of a few polls.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:14.220
If it's unexpected and the policy response
I complicated, could this spiral out of control.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.460
ALEXANDER: I have a hard time getting to that,
in part because while it-you know, we're talking

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.510
about very short timeframes.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:26.440
I mean, in some sense the two weeks before
Friday was the period when markets were, in

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.680
some sense, getting used to the notion.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:34.940
Second of all, I would argue the banking systems
is substantially better capitalized than it

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:36.340
was in 2007, 2008.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:39.820
So I think the inherent vulnerabilities around
that are less.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:46.700
I would stress the fact that nothing really
happens immediately with this vote.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.660
It's just a recommendation.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.680
As was noted, it's going to take a long time
to figure out what it actually means.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.020
And so I have no doubt markets are going to
react, including foreign exchange markets,

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:02.490
but I think-look, I would never say never,
but I don't think the right first-case assumption

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:06.000
should be some sort of systemic issue.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.630
Look, I would sort of agree with the first
set of comments, that it's, you know, hard

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.840
to know what this means in the long run.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.880
But I think it means some obvious things in
the short run.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:17.940
One is a lot of uncertainty.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:24.350
And uncertainty is generally not good for
economic activity.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:29.480
It's particularly not good for economic activity
in an environment where macro policy responses

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:30.590
are constrained.

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:35.190
So for just the issues of monetary policy,
doesn't have a lot of options; fiscal, doesn't

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.630
seem to be an option.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:42.300
So just the near-term dynamics of a significant
increase in uncertainty without a policy response

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.010
is problematic.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:50.120
I would also note that structural reform,
whatever its benefits in the long run, tends

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.500
to be negative in the short run.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:58.900
It's often hard to, I think, generate big
changes in structure, even if they are ultimately

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:05.180
positive, and orchestrate it in a way that
generates a positive effect in the short run.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:11.440
And so the kind of near-term dynamics around
whatever you think the benefits or costs are

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.420
going to be in the long run, it's hard for
me not to see it as being a challenge in the

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.420
short run.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:26.050
I do think the biggest issue outside of the
U.K. is going to be the financial spillovers.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:29.920
And that's the thing which I think is sort
of hard to predict.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:34.350
But that'll be the biggest issue outside of
the U.K.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:39.380
MALLABY: Jim, this issue of policy constraints-so,
if we think about not just the U.K. but more

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:47.690
about the euro zone, you've got not only monetary
policy which is already very active-hard to

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.720
see how it could be a lot more active.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:56.690
But also, even if you could theoretically
imagine more activism, Germany is reluctant

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:59.390
to let the European Central Bank go any further.

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:06.290
So if there was-if there was a shock, and
the shock might be Brexit or it might be something

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.740
in the Spanish election this Sunday where
the Marxist left is expected to get about

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:17.090
a quarter of the vote-in the event of that
kind of shock, the central bank is a bit constrained,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:22.150
fiscal policy is constrained by EU politics
as well.

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:30.020
You've been associated, I think it's fair,
with a view that policy is often too activist

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.240
and you should let things adjust.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.150
Do you feel comfortable with that in this
case in Europe?

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:39.000
I mean, is the right thing to admit that we
don't have policy tools all the time?

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.910
And would Europe be better off if it took
the hit and?

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:50.610
GRANT: Well, I think Europe would be better
off if the, as they are called, the authorities

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:53.080
did less rather than more.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:12:01.520
I have in mind particularly the hyper-innovationism
of the European Central Bank.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:07.480
You know, my business, my publication is called
Grant's Interest Rate Observer.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:11.220
So I speak with an interest, there are no
more interest rates to observe.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:14.000
I'm a little bit sore about this.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.020
(Laughter.)

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:17.810
But interest rates are prices.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:21.470
They are arguably the most sensitive and important
prices in capitalism.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:25.050
They define internal rates of investment.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:26.690
They help us think about risk.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:32.250
They discount future cash flows for the benefit
of investors and business people.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:43.160
And I think the way to think about this word
we use, this kind of surgically clean phrase,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:50.550
policy choices or policy tools-one way to
think about this is the-is the government

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:58.080
systematically administering prices which
arguably ought better to be discovered in

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.080
the marketplace.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:05.460
So you ask, Sebastian, about policy response
to these prospective events.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:12.740
And I think it would be blessing indeed if
some way or other, the European Central Bank

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:21.350
decided it would not impose its will on interest
rates in the euro zone.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:26.500
As it is now, the European Central Bank has
gone beyond buying sovereign debt hand over

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:35.680
fist to buying corporate bonds, such that
we see securities issued by fallible, mortal

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:42.110
corporations priced to yield less than nothing,
meaning you pay them for the privilege of

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.830
giving them your cash.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:47.390
Certainly very little is ever new under the
sun of finance.

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:48.390
This is new.

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:50.180
MALLABY: This is Unilever you're talking about?

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:54.830
GRANT: Well, there are 50-odd billion (dollars)
of security, short-dated to be sure, that

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:56.089
are being priced.

00:13:56.089 --> 00:14:05.620
I mean, as in Japan, value is no longer a
consideration in fixed-well, I was about to

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:06.620
say fixed income.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:07.680
That too is a misnomer.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:08.680
(Laughter.)

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:09.910
MALLABY: Fixed donation.

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.590
Fixed donation securities have become the
new-

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:14.590
GRANT: Yeah.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:21.440
So I think there's-I think there's a great
deformation-deforming of markets.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:25.661
And the owner of that problem, I think, or
the cause of that problem is principally the

00:14:25.661 --> 00:14:26.661
European Central Bank.

00:14:26.661 --> 00:14:31.630
MALLABY: But just to press you, so the answer
to this deformation is a reformation where

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:36.740
the European Central Bank quits buying securities,
therefore interest rates zoom up-

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:43.210
GRANT: Well, therefore interest rates are-interest
rates are allowed to go where they might go,

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:47.930
the better to discount future cash flows and
to reflect risk.

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:54.500
As it is, with sovereigns of no particular
distinction yielding about nothing before

00:14:54.500 --> 00:15:00.170
tax, and corporations are the same, how are
we to think about risk in any sensible way?

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.390
And what lies beyond is massive interventions.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:07.940
How do we-how do we refinance these debts
when they come due and need to be either paid

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:09.130
or refinanced?

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:14.580
So I think what you're talking about, Sebastian,
is the putting off of some-not necessarily

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:18.330
an evil day, but it's certainly a day of clarification.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:24.310
MALLABY: Well, maybe we should switch on that,
so we can segue to the U.S., where the Fed

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:33.620
last week indicated that it would be looser
for longer than expected some months ago.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.140
GRANT: Expected some days before.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.440
(Laughter.)

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:42.040
DINSMORE: Well, the call from Carney came
through.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:50.020
MALLABY: Emma, one thing that Yellen cited
in her press conference was actually foreign

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.980
risk, which seems to mean Brexit risk.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:59.950
If we get through this referendum on Thursday
and that risk is off the table, should Yellen

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:04.050
go back to the tighter forward path?

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:07.010
You've got-I mean, talk about where we are.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:08.550
We're at full employment.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:12.700
Is inflation heading back to trend in a way
that feels comfortable?

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:16.400
Or is there still a case for keeping rates
super low for a long time?

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:19.730
DINSMORE: So I think the challenge is you
can't look at the U.S. economy in a vacuum.

00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:24.269
So obviously the Fed's mandate has to do with
full employment and inflation, but in the

00:16:24.269 --> 00:16:32.019
context of today's global economy, with declining
GDP output across the globe and China exporting

00:16:32.019 --> 00:16:36.209
disinflation everywhere, the Fed has to be
inherently concerned about what's going on

00:16:36.209 --> 00:16:37.510
abroad.

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:41.550
The Brexit obviously provided an acute risk
for the Fed.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:47.980
The implied volatility of that if Brexit passes
could be tremendously unsettling for the U.S.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:48.980
economy.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.170
So it certainly makes sense that Yellen would
have taken a pause.

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:59.040
In terms of going forward domestically, I
think the U.S. economy as a whole looks fairly

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:00.350
decent.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:08.040
We've had a very long and somewhat lackluster-it's
not been a really exciting recovery, but we've

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.799
certainly had a recovery.

00:17:10.799 --> 00:17:15.100
And I would say, if I'm looking at his from
the Fed's perspective, I think that the U.S.

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:19.250
economic recovery is a bit long in the tooth,
in terms of just a short-term cyclical recovery.

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:23.370
And so if I'm in the Fed's position I'm a
little bit concerned about a potential downturn

00:17:23.370 --> 00:17:25.209
in the U.S. and sitting at such low rates.

00:17:25.209 --> 00:17:28.900
MALLABY: But you could just trigger the downturn
if you tighten prematurely.

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:29.900
DINSMORE: Right.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:30.900
Right.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.540
So that's the conundrum that the Fed sits
in.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:38.500
So I think that short term financial volatility
caused by external factors obviously creates

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:43.280
a situation where you do not want to be tightening
because you add to that uncertainty, you add

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:45.860
to the financial instability.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:49.010
But I think the Fed is clearly looking for
opportunities to tighten.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:54.530
I think that-I expect them to raise rates
maybe one or two more times by early next

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:57.280
year, just to give themselves some policy
room.

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:04.480
And I think if you look at the language that
Yellen used in her latest statement, much

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:08.610
less emphasis this time on inflation than
on employment.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:13.920
So in the past, Yellen has been tremendously
concerned about the headwinds to U.S. inflation-so

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.440
low oil and the strong dollar.

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:19.450
But as the base effects of those roll off
the calendar, you're now starting to really

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:23.930
believe that inflation-disinflation in the
U.S. is probably transitory.

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:29.480
And so the outlook for inflation I think,
from the Fed's perspective, is likely stabilizing.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:31.410
Obviously it has a way to go, but still stabilizing.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:35.929
So that front is a little more comforting
for the Fed.

00:18:35.929 --> 00:18:40.420
And I think that on the labor market side,
the labor market as a whole has been doing

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:41.460
fairly well.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.590
Obviously the NFP print was a shock.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:49.300
I think there's debate within the Fed about
whether or not that's statistically significant

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:50.840
or if it's noise.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:54.580
Obviously-as the economy reaches full employment,
I think there's some debate about whether

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:57.910
or not we're there, there will be some decline
in job gains.

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:00.910
It's just natural sort of course of things.

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:04.180
So I think the Fed is willing to look past
that.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:10.620
My base case scenario is that once the Brexit
risks pass, the Fed's going to look for opportunities

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:11.620
to raise rates.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:17.620
If you see any decent type of employment numbers
coming out, nothing suggesting that last month's

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:22.500
NFP was a new trend, then you should probably
see the Fed beginning to raise rates again,

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:23.640
maybe in September.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:28.770
MALLABY: So, Lewis, this idea that, you know,
the recovery is long in the tooth feels a

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:32.620
bit, well, this person is old so we should
sort of finish them off.

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:41.360
You know, so long as it's healthy-and healthy
could be defined as stable on a medium-term

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:48.460
view because inflation is 1.7 (percent), core
inflation, and it's not-it's still below target.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:56.510
And as Emma pointed out, there's lots of disinflationary
force coming from outside, notably China.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:58.780
Why not just let it continue?

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:02.540
What's the cost of just letting growth grow?

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:09.330
ALEXANDER: I think the thing the Fed is concerned
about is letting the economy get too far past

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:12.560
what you might think of as full employment
or whatnot.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:17.460
So the unemployment rate is currently 4.7,
which is actually the low end of the range

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:20.669
of the forecast for the long-term unemployment
rate.

00:20:20.669 --> 00:20:25.870
If you, in some sense, just let the economy
run and let that continue to fall, the Fed's

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:29.830
own interpretation of what drives inflation
is going to put themselves in a position where

00:20:29.830 --> 00:20:33.370
they're going to have to raise the unemployment
rate at some point down the line to keep the

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:37.280
economy from, in some sense, overheating down
the road.

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:38.660
And that's problematic.

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:41.730
And I think that's the tradeoff they're facing
right now.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:45.929
So I think we're-if you look at the latest
Fed forecast, it's sort of interesting.

00:20:45.929 --> 00:20:47.910
They have growth at potential.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:53.140
So they're-literally their forecast for growth
over the next three years are exactly at where

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:55.300
their long-run forecasts are.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:59.850
They've essentially got the unemployment rate
going down a little bit from here, but not

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:01.929
very much.

00:21:01.929 --> 00:21:06.070
And in some sense, they're predicting an economy
that is essentially moving along the full

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:07.809
employment path.

00:21:07.809 --> 00:21:11.780
And you can think about the interest rates
that they've written down as this is their

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:16.870
judgement of what is the path of interest
rates that's consistent with that outcome.

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:23.250
Now, if the economy were to, in some sense,
accelerate, I think you'd see them raise rates

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:24.250
more.

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:28.669
If the economy does not continue to generate
pressures that have the unemployment rate

00:21:28.669 --> 00:21:31.240
falling, then I think they'll sort of hold
pat.

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.460
But I think that's the sort of logic of where
they are.

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:38.060
I totally agree that there's no real urgency
here.

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:42.669
And in the wake of volatility and risk, they
are going to be pretty conservative in terms

00:21:42.669 --> 00:21:43.820
of how they transition.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:50.730
But I would totally agree that if you get
some better economic data they are on a trajectory

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:56.000
where they believe they need somewhat higher
rates ultimately to keep them on that path.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:01.540
MALLABY: Jim, there's been a couple of times
I guess in the last couple of decades where

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:08.950
the Fed has confronted a situation where price
inflation seemed to be under control, and

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:16.440
yet there were big imbalances building up
in financial markets and probably, in my view,

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:18.720
not tightening at those points was a mistake.

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:24.900
So I'm thinking of 1999 where the tech bubble
was inflating and the Fed was very slow to

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:29.990
act because it thought price inflation ain't
there, so why should we?

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:34.880
And kind of the same in 2004, 2005 with real
estate.

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:37.010
Do you see anything like that now?

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:42.650
Is there a cost to sitting there with low
rates because imbalances may be building up

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:46.080
someplace in finance that could bite us later?

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:47.600
GRANT: Without a doubt.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:54.180
Certainly they're-I mean, the Fed, since 2008,
has been implementing a policy that might

00:22:54.180 --> 00:23:01.580
be likened to putting the cart of speculation
in front of the horse of enterprise.

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:06.120
Kind of the three-dollar phrase was portfolio
balance channel.

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:14.080
What the Fed intended was that a rise in the
value of various financial assets, real estate,

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:21.210
equities and the like would induce greater
spending by the owners of assets.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:25.590
And this spending would, as it were, trickle
down-that phrase, I think, was not used-but

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:31.220
it would trickle down into other segments
of the community.

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:38.980
And we would be seeing the fruit of-well,
the outcome would be greater aggregate demand.

00:23:38.980 --> 00:23:40.960
That was the idea.

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:48.630
And what we have achieved collectively is
much higher asset prices.

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:56.289
Commercial real estate, so-called cap rates,
are very near the old highs.

00:23:56.289 --> 00:23:57.490
Interest rates you know about.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:58.919
Bond prices you know about.

00:23:58.919 --> 00:24:04.690
Equity is not quite sky-high, but certainly
treetop level.

00:24:04.690 --> 00:24:10.900
And I think the Fed is in a bit of a bind
with respect to the divergence between activity

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:15.039
on the one hand and asset prices on the other.

00:24:15.039 --> 00:24:23.630
And so the industrial economy, it seems to
me, is very near recession.

00:24:23.630 --> 00:24:26.080
Other portions of the economy, not so much.

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:32.830
But if you are in the business of engineering
a rise in assets prices, and if the earning

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:39.630
basis of those assets, as to say the real
economy, is slipping away, what do you do?

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:47.860
And by keeping interest rates low, and by
encouraging further speculation in assets

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:53.240
with borrowed money you are building yourself
the next crisis, which will entail still a

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.860
greater dose of radical monetary intervention.

00:24:55.860 --> 00:25:00.440
So yes, Sebastian, it seems to me that there
are terrific risks associated with these policies.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:06.880
Not just last FOMC meeting, but the string
of interventions going back even before the

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:09.400
crisis of 2007 and '(0)8.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:14.640
MALLABY: And we can maybe come back to that
when we go to questions, which I want to do

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:15.640
in a minute.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:19.330
But I want to get to China before we open
it up.

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:29.510
So, Emma, the concern amongst outside observers
of China used to be you guys manage your currency

00:25:29.510 --> 00:25:36.770
and you're exporting low prices, deflation,
through that route.

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:42.559
Now it is you guys have got massive overcapacity
in steel and so forth, and you are exporting

00:25:42.559 --> 00:25:46.210
deflation through a new route.

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:50.600
From China's point of view, the first strategy
worked quite well, managing the currency for

00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:51.600
a long time.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:56.039
But the overcapacity is going to be more painful
to work out.

00:25:56.039 --> 00:26:02.940
How do you think, both in the near-term and
in sort of-on a five-year view, how does China

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:08.060
work its way through this problem of enormous
amounts of industrial capacity, with lots

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:14.440
of debt associated with that, and no clear
markets for the steel they're producing?

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:15.440
DINSMORE: Right.

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:17.150
Yeah, so I think China has two choices.

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:23.090
One is to swallow the pill and make the reforms
that it needs to do to modernize its economy

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:24.919
and continue down that path.

00:26:24.919 --> 00:26:28.419
And the other is to kick the can down the
road and to continue to refinance this and

00:26:28.419 --> 00:26:32.860
continue to go back to old ways of infrastructure
investing and doing that.

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:40.390
And so I think the better outcome for China
is to move down this path of building a consumption-oriented

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:45.679
economy, and accepting the slower growth in
the short term that will come with that because

00:26:45.679 --> 00:26:50.840
ultimately the only way to work out this excess
capacity is to grow a consumer base to use

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:55.900
it and to accept the lower growth in the short
term.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:27:02.110
Obviously China-moving down that course is
very challenging for China.

00:27:02.110 --> 00:27:06.220
I think that there are a lot of economic and
political risks to them doing that, to having

00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:10.210
such a massive slowdown so quickly, and also
globally challenging.

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:14.679
So you're seeing them defray to kicking the
can down the road and continuing more of the

00:27:14.679 --> 00:27:15.679
same.

00:27:15.679 --> 00:27:22.250
But ultimately, I think China needs to start
reforming-pushing through more structural

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:28.240
reforms to create broader safety nets, social
safety nets, to encourage more saving-or,

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:34.330
I'm sorry-more consumption, and let that gradually
work through the economy.

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:38.380
Either way, China's going to export disinflation,
whether it was through their low currency

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:40.360
or through excess capacity.

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:42.580
It's going to be a fact of the global economy.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:45.890
There's not much they can do about that.

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:52.169
And really, China's best outcome longer term
is to tap into this potential-this really

00:27:52.169 --> 00:27:53.890
large potential consumer base.

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:57.100
Over the long run that's a much more sustainable
economy for them.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:02.130
It's much more stable and much more attractive,
although short run a little painful.

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:07.900
ALEXANDER: Yeah, just to build on that a little
bit, I think there's a little bit of a kind

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:10.340
of glass half full, glass half empty.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:11.340
China is slowing.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:12.340
They are doing this transition.

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:14.860
It's sort of a question of how quickly.

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:21.770
I think we've seen over the last year that
they have gone through cycles where it felt

00:28:21.770 --> 00:28:27.590
like growth was at risk, they provided some
stimulus, but at the same time not full, you

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:33.361
know, foot to the accelerator, in the way
they did in, say, 2008 and 2009.

00:28:33.361 --> 00:28:36.190
They are clearly accepting a slower path to
growth.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:40.299
And there's sort of a question of does the
transition path work?

00:28:40.299 --> 00:28:48.000
Look, I think the domestic financial problem,
which is essentially the financial counterpart

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:55.800
to the sectors that have this excess capacity
is going to be a challenge to manage domestically.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:29:01.120
I worry about it in a context where China
is opening up in certain ways financially,

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:05.960
which is making themselves more vulnerable
to market dynamics and finance.

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:08.380
And that's an inherently challenging thing.

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:11.170
I think they have the fiscal capacity to manage
it.

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:13.309
Essentially the financial system is fully
backed.

00:29:13.309 --> 00:29:16.070
But it is-it is going to be a tricky thing.

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:22.450
And they've got a large asset quality problem
in their banking system, which is going to

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:25.309
take them many years to sort of work through.

00:29:25.309 --> 00:29:31.500
But you know, I don't think it's obvious that
it isn't manageable.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:36.790
I do think the overcapacity, what it implies
for trade going forward, is something that

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:39.840
we're going to hear more about and have to
worry about.

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:43.820
Just anybody who's looked at the history of
the steel industry is the history of the steel

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:50.080
industry globally is global excess capacity
leading to trade problems, right?

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:53.840
In the sort of '60s it was excess capacity
in Europe.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:59.580
In the '70s and '80s it was excess capacity
in Japan.

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:05.620
And now-and both of those problems are dwarfed
by the situation in China.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:09.830
And so that is going to be something that
we are all going to have to manage.

00:30:09.830 --> 00:30:14.890
And in an environment where, frankly, I think
trade is going to be a bigger part of the

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:16.159
agenda going forward, it is going to be-you
know, it's something we're going to have to

00:30:16.159 --> 00:30:18.100
be dealing with.

00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:19.990
MALLABY: Right.

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:26.919
The politics of the West are not such that
absorbing a surge in cheap manufactures is

00:30:26.919 --> 00:30:27.970
politically easy.

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:29.030
ALEXANDER: Yeah.

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:33.039
MALLABY: At this point, let's invite members
to join the conversation.

00:30:33.039 --> 00:30:34.640
Remember, this is on the record.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:40.110
So if you have a question wait for the microphone
and I will call on you.

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:41.280
One right here, yes.

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:42.280
Coming up.

00:30:42.280 --> 00:30:43.919
Q: Yeah, thank you.

00:30:43.919 --> 00:30:47.650
Juan Ocampo with-(comes on mic)-Can you hear
me?-Trajectory Asset Management.

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:51.750
Jim, I got a question for you about an interest
rate that cannot be observed, the so-called

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:57.980
natural rate that John Williams of the Fed
has written about for a long time, et cetera.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:04.080
As you know, 30 years ago that rate, as they
calculate it, was 500 basis points, and now

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:07.150
it's-they tend to calculate it around zero.

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:15.169
And it's being used as something that interventionists,
as you would say, for very loose monetary

00:31:15.169 --> 00:31:20.090
policy say, well, we have to keep it very
low because the natural rate is low.

00:31:20.090 --> 00:31:26.200
The question for you is, do you think the
rate-the natural rate is so low because of

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:31.870
this ongoing intervention that's been going
on for so many years that the patient, essentially,

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:34.990
is showing a different symptom profile?

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:36.510
GRANT: Well, I do.

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:41.450
One shouldn't dogmatize about an interest
rate that exists only in the imagination of

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:44.250
the mandarins of monetary policy.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:50.669
Actually, there is an extensive literature
on the natural rate that antedates Knut Wicksell,

00:31:50.669 --> 00:31:52.779
who died in, I think, 1926.

00:31:52.779 --> 00:32:00.390
The so-called Austrian school, now mostly
associated with Hayek and Ludwig von Mises,

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:06.120
talked about a natural rate at which the demand
for savings was equal to the supply force

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:12.340
savings, not bank credit force-fed into the
system through unique and heretofore unimaginable

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:16.120
central bank actions, but rather savings-deferred
consumption.

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:22.539
So I think that the notion of the-of this
invisible friend to the central banks called

00:32:22.539 --> 00:32:25.960
the natural rate, this secret, invisible friend.

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:27.490
This is a kind of a contrivance.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:35.980
I think it is an intellectual contrivance
that is used in argument to justify these

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:41.160
increasingly grotesque interventions.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:44.760
And The Wall Street Journal reported on the
so-called natural rate.

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:50.500
And somebody was, you know, it's-you can't
see it, but you can infer its existence.

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:52.760
It's like the planet Pluto.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:53.760
(Laughter.)

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:56.850
And I was thinking, no.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:58.910
They have not discovered Pluto.

00:32:58.910 --> 00:33:01.400
Rather, they have blundered onto Goofy.

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:02.400
(Laughter.)

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:05.720
ALEXANDER: Can I jump in here?

00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:06.950
MALLABY: Sure.

00:33:06.950 --> 00:33:11.840
ALEXANDER: Look, you can make this concept
very artificial and whatnot, but ultimately

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:17.920
it comes back to a very simple question, which
is: What is the relationship basically between

00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:20.570
growth and interest rates?

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:24.840
This whole debate about the natural rate and
how you measure it is really just looking

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:26.020
at that.

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:27.360
Let's look at the last six months.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:32.929
The last six months, the U.S. economy has
grown at about 1.1, 1.2 percent, with real

00:33:32.929 --> 00:33:36.740
rates that are essentially zero.

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:42.559
I would argue that 1.1 is almost certainly
below anybody's estimate of what potential

00:33:42.559 --> 00:33:43.559
is.

00:33:43.559 --> 00:33:47.600
So the normal definition of the neutral or
natural rate that is commonly used in the

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:52.360
monetary policy debate is what is the level
of interest rates that gets the economy to

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:53.900
grow at potential?

00:33:53.900 --> 00:33:59.760
It's a very simple observation to say that
whatever that rate is, it's in some sense

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:03.010
lower than where rates are now.

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:08.089
And when the Fed talks about that, that's
ultimately the issue they're talking about.

00:34:08.089 --> 00:34:11.789
And I think when you-when you seriously try
and look at how the economy's behaved over

00:34:11.789 --> 00:34:19.200
the last five years, it's hard not to draw
the conclusion that that relationship between

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:22.899
interest rates and growth is very different
from what we had before.

00:34:22.899 --> 00:34:27.540
Now, I think what you're seeing in the discussion
around these issues within the Fed is struggling

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:33.770
to understand why that's the case and to try
to project forward, you know, how is that

00:34:33.770 --> 00:34:35.349
going to happen going forward?

00:34:35.349 --> 00:34:39.679
I would argue part of the problem with the
Fed over the last five years has been they

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:42.360
have been too optimistic about the recovery.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:47.929
What you've seen consistently is them overestimating
growth and overestimating the path of rates

00:34:47.929 --> 00:34:50.120
that the economy could accommodate.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:54.300
Ultimately, that is about the path of this
very simple concept.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:57.000
Going forward, what's going to matter?

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:00.350
I think the simple way to think about the
way the Fed is going to behave is they're

00:35:00.350 --> 00:35:03.400
going to see how the economy responds.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:09.490
If the economy picks up, that implies that
in some sense it can handle higher rates and

00:35:09.490 --> 00:35:10.780
they can raise rates.

00:35:10.780 --> 00:35:13.910
If it does not, they won't.

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:16.250
It's really that simple.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:19.560
And in some sense, you can-you can make this
a bigger deal than it is.

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:23.400
But ultimately it's about the very simple
relationship between growth and the level

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:24.560
of rates.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:29.110
DINSMORE: And I think on that point, in terms
of the Fed overestimating growth, I think

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:33.550
at the end of the day economic growth as a
whole boils down to confidence.

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:37.320
And if people are confident enough to invest,
if businesses are confident to invest, then

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:38.770
you start seeing that.

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:43.190
And I think that a lot of the Fed's-I would
agree the Fed has been overly optimistic.

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:47.900
But I imagine the counterfactual, in which
Janet Yellen says that she was tremendously

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:52.240
concerned about the outlook for the U.S. economy
and they didn't have this level of optimism

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:56.510
baked in, I think that would be disastrous
for confidence.

00:35:56.510 --> 00:36:00.680
And I think that that also goes back to the
question about exceptional monetary policy,

00:36:00.680 --> 00:36:03.350
and we talked about that in the ECB and the
euro.

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:10.080
I wonder in the absence of the ECBs intervention,
which I agree has been quite unprecedented

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:14.160
and exceptional, what really would the European
economy look like?

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:15.160
GRANT: Yeah.

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:18.520
Well, we talked about growth, which is an
income statement concept.

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:24.690
But we also ought to talk about leverage,
which is a balance sheet concept.

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:30.560
And if the Fed insists on pressing down rates,
or at least not lifting up rates, or doing

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:38.180
what it's doing, I guess, is the way to think
about its policy-if the Fed insists on persisting,

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:42.340
it seems to me we will see a great deal more
of what we are currently witnessing, namely

00:36:42.340 --> 00:36:51.349
the incurring of debt to generate income that
is not there in the natural level, or the

00:36:51.349 --> 00:36:56.560
observed level of interest rates, the nominal
rates.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:02.849
What you don't see is what is going on with
the balance sheet side of things.

00:37:02.849 --> 00:37:08.570
You can observe it at intervals, but I think
there is a preoccupation on growth to the

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:17.119
detriment of our awareness of the dangers
that heightened leverage to induce-or produce

00:37:17.119 --> 00:37:23.730
income artificially, what that goes to finance,
and the kind of crises it introduces and the

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:26.210
kind of responses to those crises.

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:29.570
MALLABY: Another question?

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:35.170
Otherwise I've got a question.

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:38.543
Oh, I see one over here, OK.

00:37:38.543 --> 00:37:39.543
Here, and then you.

00:37:39.543 --> 00:37:40.543
Q: One of you observed that-
MALLABY: Could you just identify-

00:37:40.543 --> 00:37:41.543
Q: I'm sorry.

00:37:41.543 --> 00:37:42.970
Arthur Rubin with SMBC Nikko.

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:48.470
One of you observed that if Brexit were to
occur, that only would the pound head down,

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:51.930
but it would probably have a negative impact
against the euro.

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:57.220
There's a finite number of things that they
can go down against, which implies an even

00:37:57.220 --> 00:37:58.850
stronger dollar.

00:37:58.850 --> 00:38:03.830
The Fed-various members of the Fed have been
vociferous in saying that they're not going

00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:08.130
to make their decisions based on what's happening
to the dollar in the rest of the world, yet

00:38:08.130 --> 00:38:14.210
it's hard not to believe that at some level
there's kind of a veto over what the Fed does

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:16.100
by where the dollar is.

00:38:16.100 --> 00:38:22.349
Do you think that a Brexit should occur, and
if it did have this knock-on effect on both

00:38:22.349 --> 00:38:27.651
the euro and the pound, what does that mean
for the Fed's decision making policy and the

00:38:27.651 --> 00:38:29.619
impact of the dollar rates?

00:38:29.619 --> 00:38:30.619
For anybody.

00:38:30.619 --> 00:38:35.580
DINSMORE: Well, I think if that happens you're
going to see easier monetary policy across

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.730
the board.

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:41.890
No country is supposed to-no central bank
is supposed to explicitly target their currency

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:47.300
as a policy lever, but obviously currencies
influence inflation.

00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:49.410
And so it can't be ignored.

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:57.900
A stronger dollar is one of the biggest headwinds,
I think-or potential upside risks-or downside

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:00.830
risks of inflation in the U.S.

00:39:00.830 --> 00:39:06.940
And you would most certainly see concerns-or,
renewed concerns about the inflation outlook

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:12.780
for the U.S. if the dollar breaks much higher
from here.

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:17.119
And really, I think, as you look at the Fed's
concerns about inflation-so their two concerns

00:39:17.119 --> 00:39:21.340
about inflation had been low oil prices and
a strong dollar.

00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:25.670
And the base effects of those on a calendar
year basis are scheduled to roll off, and

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:27.490
so you actually should see some inflationary
pressures.

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:34.080
But if you see the dollar spike again, that's
going to renew this disinflationary concern,

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:39.670
and certainly I think would prompt Fed members
to be hesitant to raise rates.

00:39:39.670 --> 00:39:41.580
MALLABY: Teresa.

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:49.010
Q: Teresa Barger from Cartica Capital, visiting
from Washington, D.C.

00:39:49.010 --> 00:39:53.490
I would just love it if you could talk about
two things.

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:55.640
One is productivity.

00:39:55.640 --> 00:40:01.630
Pre-GFC, it was growing for 50 years at 2.5
percent, and since at 0.5 percent.

00:40:01.630 --> 00:40:03.780
That's a U.S. number.

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:08.000
I don't-but similar in Europe and similar
in the emerging markets.

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:11.030
We've had a real stepdown in productivity.

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:14.410
And where do you see we might get out of that?

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:17.600
And the related question is with margins.

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:19.180
Corporate margins have come down.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:25.070
I, of course, only know the emerging markets,
but it's also true in the U.S.

00:40:25.070 --> 00:40:29.950
And are we seeing any green shoots of an uptick?

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:31.359
And where will that come from?

00:40:31.359 --> 00:40:33.339
MALLABY: Go ahead then.

00:40:33.339 --> 00:40:36.810
ALEXANDER: I'm happy to talk about productivity.

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:39.470
First of all, I would question a little bit
the way you characterize it.

00:40:39.470 --> 00:40:43.140
When you said 2.5 percent before the global
financial crisis, it's-

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:44.870
Q: Over 50 years too.

00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:49.050
ALEXANDER: Well, but what you, in fact, had
was relatively high productivity growth until

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:50.260
around the mid-'70s.

00:40:50.260 --> 00:40:52.030
It then fell quite dramatically.

00:40:52.030 --> 00:40:57.320
We had a little bit of a recovery starting
in the mid-'90s that lasted for a decade or

00:40:57.320 --> 00:40:58.320
less.

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:03.810
And that's kind of where you get that average,
but in fact it's not literally constant.

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:07.869
In some ways what's happened is we've come
back to those lower levels we saw in the '70s

00:41:07.869 --> 00:41:14.130
and, frankly, lower levels that we saw before
the 1920s.

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:19.190
This is-there's a lot of interesting work
that's going on on this subject at this time.

00:41:19.190 --> 00:41:24.119
There's a book by Bob Gordon, who I-it's a
little too long.

00:41:24.119 --> 00:41:25.900
It could have used an editor.

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:29.359
But it's actually a very good book.

00:41:29.359 --> 00:41:33.780
I think there are good reasons to expect low
productivity growth as something where it's

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:35.670
here to stay.

00:41:35.670 --> 00:41:39.360
It's not that there aren't positive things
going on, but I think they're less broad than

00:41:39.360 --> 00:41:41.119
they used to be.

00:41:41.119 --> 00:41:48.470
One of the simple ways to think about it is
as wonderful as IT is, and lord knows that

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:54.770
is the thing that we're living with, how many
people are actually-how many workers are made

00:41:54.770 --> 00:42:00.420
better by IT, versus how many workers are
effectively substitutes to IT?

00:42:00.420 --> 00:42:04.680
When you think about the technologies that
drove those previous waves of innovation-things

00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:08.980
like electricity and the internal combustion
engine-one of the characteristics of those

00:42:08.980 --> 00:42:11.640
things was they made everybody.

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:14.460
And I think we're living in a world where
that's much less true.

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:17.359
And so I think it's reason to be less optimistic.

00:42:17.359 --> 00:42:24.520
As I look at the data, I think there's very
little evidence to expect an uptick.

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:30.460
And so my expectations are built on the judgment
that I'm not expecting productivity to accelerate.

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:33.620
That's not to say that it couldn't happen.

00:42:33.620 --> 00:42:36.900
Frankly, economists are lousy at predicting
productivity.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:41.109
So we should say that very clearly.

00:42:41.109 --> 00:42:45.590
But I do think a low productivity world is
part of what we're living with.

00:42:45.590 --> 00:42:49.500
When you look around the world, at emerging
markets and others, what you generally see

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:52.100
is slowing productivity everywhere.

00:42:52.100 --> 00:42:54.600
It's a little bit of a different story.

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:59.760
The stories you would tell for the U.S., which
we think of as being kind of at the frontier,

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:03.500
is a little bit different than what you would
think of as an emerging economy that presumably

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:07.840
is not at the frontier and ought to be able
to, in some sense, generate stronger productivity

00:43:07.840 --> 00:43:09.890
growth by moving towards the frontier.

00:43:09.890 --> 00:43:13.530
I think you have to tell kind of different
stories.

00:43:13.530 --> 00:43:18.110
But it is certainly true that the data would
suggest that this slowdown in productivity

00:43:18.110 --> 00:43:20.810
is a near universal phenomenon.

00:43:20.810 --> 00:43:23.930
And that is part of the problem of the world
we live in.

00:43:23.930 --> 00:43:27.070
I think we are living in a world where growth
is slow.

00:43:27.070 --> 00:43:36.320
And I think I would-I certainly feel like
monetary policy is, like, not the best too-and

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:39.579
I would argue it's the worst tool to address
that.

00:43:39.579 --> 00:43:43.500
It may, of course, be the only one we can
use at the moment, which is kind of a different

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:44.500
problem.

00:43:44.500 --> 00:43:47.130
But there's no question that there are other
things that we ought to be doing to try to

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:48.130
and address this.

00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:52.470
And the frustration is it's just pick your
country, you tell a different story for why

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:54.160
that's not happening.

00:43:54.160 --> 00:43:58.130
MALLABY: And maybe, Emma, we could pursue
this a bit with you.

00:43:58.130 --> 00:44:00.010
Because I think, I mean, Teresa had two questions.

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:02.880
And the other one was about corporate margins.

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:07.670
And maybe they're actually linked, in the
sense that some of what's going on with new

00:44:07.670 --> 00:44:12.930
technology is stuff gets produced which is
valuable to consumers, the apps on my iPhone

00:44:12.930 --> 00:44:18.490
and so forth, for which the price-the explicit
price is zero.

00:44:18.490 --> 00:44:22.840
So it doesn't show up in a productivity gain
from the company that makes it, nor does it

00:44:22.840 --> 00:44:23.950
produce a profit.

00:44:23.950 --> 00:44:28.430
It may produce a welfare gain, but we're not
measuring it.

00:44:28.430 --> 00:44:30.560
And maybe that's the linking factor in these
two questions.

00:44:30.560 --> 00:44:31.560
DINSMORE: Well, I think it does.

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:35.580
I think in the global economy, technology
has really thrown a wrench into how we calculate

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:37.390
productivity and how we think about it.

00:44:37.390 --> 00:44:42.790
And I'm not sure to the extent that technology
accounts for what percentage of the decrease

00:44:42.790 --> 00:44:46.930
in productivity growth that technology accounts
for, but it certainly has to account for some

00:44:46.930 --> 00:44:52.710
of it just because we simply don't understand
the true monetary value of data.

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:57.359
And data flows are skyrocketing globally.

00:44:57.359 --> 00:45:01.550
And companies don't necessarily know what
they can monetize off of data.

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:05.960
Governments don't necessarily know what value
they're getting from it.

00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:11.000
And it's hard to really derive any kind of
productivity or economic value to data other

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:15.040
than to see that data flows are tremendously
higher.

00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:20.619
And I think the question for economists over
the next few years is how do we quantify that,

00:45:20.619 --> 00:45:25.570
and what does that mean for global productivity
and how we think about growth, and what's

00:45:25.570 --> 00:45:27.250
kind of the next wave?

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:33.970
I think it opens a lot of potential doors,
particularly in the realm of structural reforms

00:45:33.970 --> 00:45:39.060
and innovations for economies, because in
some ways data is the great equalizer.

00:45:39.060 --> 00:45:44.490
You know, if I have a smartphone I can do
things that I could never do with ten other

00:45:44.490 --> 00:45:45.640
devices.

00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:51.670
So I think that's going to open a lot of doors
for technological advancements and machination

00:45:51.670 --> 00:45:55.820
across a lot of emerging markets, which could
be a really hopeful prospect for the global

00:45:55.820 --> 00:45:56.820
economy.

00:45:56.820 --> 00:46:01.829
But in the short term, really, there has to
be a lot of cohesion about what to do.

00:46:01.829 --> 00:46:06.940
And as we mentioned right now, the main policy
response has been monetary policy.

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:11.060
And there has been a real disappointing lack
of anything else on the front of structural

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:13.619
reforms or fiscal policy to really push that
through.

00:46:13.619 --> 00:46:17.310
MALLABY: But let me just suggest one analogy,
and see if any of you want to pick up on it.

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:24.720
So maybe this is sort of a Japan 1980s type
of thing, where, you know, the Japanese were

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:29.690
both-you know, the kind of-the stereotype
about Japanese business was it goes for market

00:46:29.690 --> 00:46:36.480
share, it's great at getting market share,
it doesn't have enormous margins, super competitive.

00:46:36.480 --> 00:46:42.660
And in some sense, technological shifts has
driven a lot of businesses, it seems to me,

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:46.800
into a mind frame where they want to get a
lot of eyeballs, they know that technology

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:50.510
creates lots of network effects if you're
the first mover, you have to create these

00:46:50.510 --> 00:46:51.510
platforms.

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:55.220
You monetize down the road, a long time down
the road.

00:46:55.220 --> 00:47:00.560
And in the meantime, you're accepting thing
margins, and you're not-you know, you're not

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:06.930
making consumers pay for what you're doing,
and you're willing to create both the effects

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:12.280
that you're talking about, both low corporate
margins and low measured productivity.

00:47:12.280 --> 00:47:15.339
Does that-
GRANT: Yeah, they're not only low margins,

00:47:15.339 --> 00:47:19.990
but nonexistent margins in the case-famously
in the case of the many unicorns in Silicon

00:47:19.990 --> 00:47:21.440
Valley.

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:27.690
Notice that zero percent interest rates allow
the suspension, as it were, of disbelief about

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:31.430
future earnings.

00:47:31.430 --> 00:47:38.319
And with respect to the measurement of productivity,
one is reminded of the difficulties of measuring

00:47:38.319 --> 00:47:39.910
many economic phenomena.

00:47:39.910 --> 00:47:44.770
There was a wonderful book published about-I
guess more than 50 years ago, Oskar Morgenstern,

00:47:44.770 --> 00:47:46.900
on the accuracy of economic observations.

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:54.010
And he absolutely rips into the newspapers
of the day with their credulous reporting

00:47:54.010 --> 00:47:58.760
of these data from the Commerce Department
to the right of the decimal place.

00:47:58.760 --> 00:47:59.970
Can you?

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:04.380
And here we are talking about whether the
rate the inflation that the Fed is targeting

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:13.200
is 1.7 or 1.8, bearing in mind, for example,
that YouTube is for free.

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:20.620
I mean, how exactly-what precision do we expect
of these measurements?

00:48:20.620 --> 00:48:24.840
And are we quite right in being, as Janet
Yellen is wont to say, data dependent?

00:48:24.840 --> 00:48:29.460
I think the thing to know about Ms. Yellen,
among other things, is that she gets to the

00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:31.569
airport three hours early for a flight.

00:48:31.569 --> 00:48:32.569
(Laughter.)

00:48:32.569 --> 00:48:33.710
She is not an impulsive person.

00:48:33.710 --> 00:48:35.650
And perhaps she is right to be data dependent.

00:48:35.650 --> 00:48:40.510
But one must think a little bit about the
accuracy of the data on which one depends.

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:41.770
MALLABY: Lewis.

00:48:41.770 --> 00:48:45.050
ALEXANDER: So let me-let me kind of say a
couple things.

00:48:45.050 --> 00:48:49.400
First of all, corporate profits are at record
highs.

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:52.930
All right, so if you look at the corporate
profit share, it's come down a little bit

00:48:52.930 --> 00:48:56.810
over the last year, but the basic story of
the last 20 years has been rising corporate

00:48:56.810 --> 00:48:57.810
profit shares.

00:48:57.810 --> 00:49:04.560
So, yes, there are issues in the tech sector,
but it is not a generic problem for the economy

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:05.560
overall-number one.

00:49:05.560 --> 00:49:12.100
Number two, one of the things to just be aware
of-and, look, I spent three years working

00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:13.540
for the Department of Commerce.

00:49:13.540 --> 00:49:17.680
So I was the chief economist at the Department
of Commerce, and sat over all these things.

00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:22.339
And trust me, I understand full well the difficulties
in doing this.

00:49:22.339 --> 00:49:24.910
GDP is not reality.

00:49:24.910 --> 00:49:26.910
It's just a concept and it's an estimate.

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:30.030
We put way too much emphasis on it.

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:35.280
However, there are certain things that all
these numbers are benchmarked to.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:42.070
And for example, all of our basically aggregate
measures are benchmarked to tax returns.

00:49:42.070 --> 00:49:49.760
So if the question is, are we mis-measuring
productivity because we're not capturing income,

00:49:49.760 --> 00:49:55.630
you have to believe-and if that's going to
explain the slowdown in productivity growth,

00:49:55.630 --> 00:49:58.900
you have to believe that tax evasion has gone
up.

00:49:58.900 --> 00:50:05.410
Now, I'm prepared to accept that it's gone
up some, but the notion that it's gone up

00:50:05.410 --> 00:50:09.710
dramatically is kind of hard for me to get
my head around.

00:50:09.710 --> 00:50:16.710
Alternatively, we know that our concepts of
output in big parts of the economy are just

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:18.869
very, very weak.

00:50:18.869 --> 00:50:21.390
So a big part of our economy is services.

00:50:21.390 --> 00:50:26.210
To be perfectly frankly, it's a lot easier
to measure physical stuff, right?

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:30.530
When the economy was dominated by manufacturing,
it was a lot easier to measure because you

00:50:30.530 --> 00:50:34.060
measure how much steel comes out of steel
plants, you can measure, you know, how man

00:50:34.060 --> 00:50:36.140
locomotives come out of the factories.

00:50:36.140 --> 00:50:41.270
When you start talking about the output of,
say, the health care sector of the financial

00:50:41.270 --> 00:50:45.440
sector, our concepts are a lot harder.

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:51.540
And so in some aggregate sense there's a huge
amount of uncertainty and mis-measurement

00:50:51.540 --> 00:50:53.079
around these numbers.

00:50:53.079 --> 00:50:55.849
But that's not really the question.

00:50:55.849 --> 00:51:00.880
The question is what we are seeing is a notable
slowdown in productivity.

00:51:00.880 --> 00:51:05.330
And to, in some sense, believe that that is
because of mis-measurement, you have to believe

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:10.220
that all of those problems have gotten materially
worse.

00:51:10.220 --> 00:51:15.359
And I would argue the serious people who have
gone out and asked that question come and

00:51:15.359 --> 00:51:21.099
say: You can explain some of it, but not most
of it.

00:51:21.099 --> 00:51:29.340
Most of what we're dealing with in the global
economy is a weak growth in nominal income.

00:51:29.340 --> 00:51:35.099
And that's-you know, that is something where
the measurement issues are just much, much,

00:51:35.099 --> 00:51:36.589
much smaller.

00:51:36.589 --> 00:51:41.560
Most of this measurement stuff comes down
to the distinction between nominal and real.

00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:45.650
Our ability to measure nominal incomes are
a lot better.

00:51:45.650 --> 00:51:50.650
And what-the problem, I would argue, that
we're all struggling with is a nominal income

00:51:50.650 --> 00:51:51.849
problem.

00:51:51.849 --> 00:51:55.460
MALLABY: Another question?

00:51:55.460 --> 00:51:59.069
Yes, right here.

00:51:59.069 --> 00:52:04.220
Q: I'm Kenneth Bialkin with Skadden Arps.

00:52:04.220 --> 00:52:12.260
When we hear your discussion, it's clearly
premised on a collective objective of improving

00:52:12.260 --> 00:52:19.960
our economy, our employment, and the condition
of the American people, consumers and workers.

00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:26.980
What I'm confused about is when we talk about
China, which you've talked about a little

00:52:26.980 --> 00:52:35.500
bit, when China's rulers, who are not necessarily
responsible to their voters, look at what

00:52:35.500 --> 00:52:45.710
to do with the U.S. economy in terms of their
activity-selling, buying, moving, not moving-they

00:52:45.710 --> 00:52:48.569
don't care about American productivity.

00:52:48.569 --> 00:52:53.510
They care about the interests that are interesting
to them.

00:52:53.510 --> 00:53:02.730
My question to you is, on the decisions made
by the rulers of China as to how to act with

00:53:02.730 --> 00:53:09.319
relationship to the American economy, are
they favoring, are they interested, let's

00:53:09.319 --> 00:53:13.020
stay, in the employment rate of the Chinese
worker?

00:53:13.020 --> 00:53:18.670
Are they interested in the welfare of the
Chinese consumer?

00:53:18.670 --> 00:53:26.880
Or are they focused more primarily on political
or other interests having to do with their

00:53:26.880 --> 00:53:28.390
longer-range prospects?

00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:35.970
In other words, a difference between China
and America in this conversation that we're

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:38.560
having, how do you evaluate that?

00:53:38.560 --> 00:53:41.700
MALLABY: Well, OK, maybe let's go to Jim first.

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:49.599
I mean, I think what strikes me about the
question is that it is true that in a non-market

00:53:49.599 --> 00:53:54.609
economy with a lot of state direction the
objection of certain patterns of trading and

00:53:54.609 --> 00:53:56.119
so forth may be political.

00:53:56.119 --> 00:54:03.359
So when you see a wave of Chinese external
investment buying up German robotics companies

00:54:03.359 --> 00:54:08.650
or American hotel chains, is this purely a
market transaction, they want to make a profit,

00:54:08.650 --> 00:54:10.750
or is there some political agenda?

00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:11.750
GRANT: Yeah.

00:54:11.750 --> 00:54:16.750
Well, with all the humility of someone who
lives not in China, but rather in Brooklyn,

00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:19.140
let me propose a couple of thoughts.

00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:27.359
One is there appears to be a rather urgent
movement of funds out of China.

00:54:27.359 --> 00:54:36.050
My second observation is that I propose to
you that China might be fairly regarded as

00:54:36.050 --> 00:54:50.640
a kind of financial dystopia, in which the
state sets the rules principally for the benefit

00:54:50.640 --> 00:54:57.339
of those already in power, namely the Communist
Party and the friends of the Communist Party.

00:54:57.339 --> 00:55:07.660
And I think that China is-financially speaking-is
the world's biggest problem.

00:55:07.660 --> 00:55:17.140
And I suggest to you that one day we will
wake up and hear or read that there has been

00:55:17.140 --> 00:55:24.579
a collapse in the wealth management products
of the Chinese shadow banking and banking

00:55:24.579 --> 00:55:26.869
systems.

00:55:26.869 --> 00:55:31.589
These wealth management products are closed
and, indeed, blind pools.

00:55:31.589 --> 00:55:32.849
One doesn't know what's in them.

00:55:32.849 --> 00:55:38.859
They principally invest in credit instruments-bonds
and the like.

00:55:38.859 --> 00:55:40.710
Three-point-six trillion (dollars') worth.

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:49.059
In a $10 1/2 trillion economy these things
are growing $4(00) or $500 billion a year.

00:55:49.059 --> 00:55:55.030
The principal technique for the redemption
of wealth management products is the issuance

00:55:55.030 --> 00:55:56.960
of new wealth management products.

00:55:56.960 --> 00:56:02.410
And the word Ponzi was in fact applied to
this phenomenon by a leading Chinese banker

00:56:02.410 --> 00:56:04.430
a couple of years ago.

00:56:04.430 --> 00:56:10.740
A couple of years ago means that it has been
going on, and kept going on, and defying the

00:56:10.740 --> 00:56:14.119
bears who see in it-as do I. I certainly am
bearish on this.

00:56:14.119 --> 00:56:16.940
I see the world's greatest financial risk.

00:56:16.940 --> 00:56:19.950
So, sir, you asked what the Chinese want.

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:21.569
I think they want to remain in power.

00:56:21.569 --> 00:56:23.390
The rulers want to remain in power.

00:56:23.390 --> 00:56:27.230
They are interested in the manipulation of
information to that end.

00:56:27.230 --> 00:56:31.450
They are interested in the manipulation of
markets to that end.

00:56:31.450 --> 00:56:35.630
And I think that what is going on within China
financially is very, very worrying.

00:56:35.630 --> 00:56:42.150
DINSMORE: I think I would add onto that, if
you look at the history of China since 1979,

00:56:42.150 --> 00:56:47.109
the rise in Chinese soft power globally has
been economically driven.

00:56:47.109 --> 00:56:52.640
And for the Chinese government, the economy
is one of its most powerful tools.

00:56:52.640 --> 00:56:55.810
No one would be talking about China if China
didn't have the extraordinary growth rate

00:56:55.810 --> 00:56:58.869
that it has had over the last few years.

00:56:58.869 --> 00:57:03.270
And so I believe that it is in the interest
of the Chinese party to stay in power.

00:57:03.270 --> 00:57:08.270
And to stay in power, it is in their interest
to keep their economy growing, at least at

00:57:08.270 --> 00:57:13.579
some rate that doesn't cause social unrest,
which is why I think that they end up kicking

00:57:13.579 --> 00:57:17.290
the can down the road and they don't want
to agitate anything in the economy, and try

00:57:17.290 --> 00:57:19.410
to be so cautious in that.

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:24.790
I think politically that creates a-starting
to get out of my realm of expertise as an

00:57:24.790 --> 00:57:31.069
economist-but politically it creates an interesting
backdrop for the Communist Party, where as

00:57:31.069 --> 00:57:37.349
the economy slows, there are increasing calls
for more representation from the people.

00:57:37.349 --> 00:57:42.360
And I think that you're starting to see some
concerns among the Party members about their

00:57:42.360 --> 00:57:45.970
own hold on power as the economy slows.

00:57:45.970 --> 00:57:50.550
So you've seen a lot of finger pointing, a
lot of dissension within the Party.

00:57:50.550 --> 00:57:58.200
And so, for me, I think if you're looking
at the outlook for China, there's an inextricable

00:57:58.200 --> 00:58:04.290
link between economic stability and the ability
for them to continue under the one party system.

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:05.290
MALLABY: Yeah.

00:58:05.290 --> 00:58:07.339
And last word for Lewis.

00:58:07.339 --> 00:58:09.099
ALEXANDER: Just three quick things very quickly.

00:58:09.099 --> 00:58:13.079
First of all, I total agree that the biggest
risk we face in the globe the Chinese financial

00:58:13.079 --> 00:58:15.589
system, and the risk of a problem there.

00:58:15.589 --> 00:58:17.119
That's number one.

00:58:17.119 --> 00:58:22.670
Number two, the material improvement in the
lives of the Chinese people over the last

00:58:22.670 --> 00:58:29.760
30 years is probably the single greatest improvement
like that we've-in human history.

00:58:29.760 --> 00:58:33.800
So whatever else you think about what the
Chinese are doing, I think you kind of have

00:58:33.800 --> 00:58:39.900
to acknowledge that this is the greatest economic
success story for the broadest number of people

00:58:39.900 --> 00:58:40.900
we've ever experienced.

00:58:40.900 --> 00:58:46.470
The third thing I would just say is, it seems
to me it's on us to manage our relationship

00:58:46.470 --> 00:58:49.430
with China in way that's good for us.

00:58:49.430 --> 00:58:53.070
And that I don't know.

00:58:53.070 --> 00:58:56.040
You know, one of the problems, frankly, with
China, one of the things I worry about, is

00:58:56.040 --> 00:58:58.569
we do not understand how they make decisions.

00:58:58.569 --> 00:59:01.750
So it's hard-it's hard to answer your question
directly.

00:59:01.750 --> 00:59:08.079
But ultimately, it's on us to make sure that
our economic relationship with China is one

00:59:08.079 --> 00:59:09.450
that's productive for us.

00:59:09.450 --> 00:59:10.570
MALLABY: OK.

00:59:10.570 --> 00:59:14.020
So from Brexit to China, it's been a great
sprint around the world.

00:59:14.020 --> 00:59:16.451
Thank you to all panelists and thank you for
coming out.

00:59:16.451 --> 00:59:17.451
(Applause.)

00:59:17.451 --> 00:59:17.457
(END)
This is an uncorrected transcript.

